On Mondays, I work on the labour ward from 8am. I check on the progress of the women in labour, and those in the small high-dependency unit who have had complications. We did two elective caesarean sections. Both were fine. It was quite a quiet day, but there are times when it gets to 6pm and you haven't sat down.
I decided to become an anaesthetist after being present during a caesarean as a medical student. The woman was awake when her baby was born, and I thought that was brilliant.
On Monday night I was on call for the intensive care unit [ICU], so I went from seeing fit and healthy women giving birth, to looking after the sickest patients in the hospital. I was responsible for 12 patients – some of whom I know quite well because they have been in for a while. I live only about 10 minutes from the hospital, so I got home about 9.30pm, though it's not unknown to have to spend all night there. I was called at 2am, to discuss admitting a patient who had had emergency abdominal surgery – elderly patients are kept asleep for longer after an operation, and then brought to the ICU to be monitored more closely.
I don't see patients on Tuesday mornings, so I can have a lie-in. That afternoon, we had a meeting with all the consultants, nurses and the palliative care team to discuss patients we are concerned about. One was a patient who was very sick, but wasn't able to communicate, and had had mental health problems. Other patients are often unconscious or sedated. What I may think would be best is not necessarily what the patient would want. It's something we take very seriously. We think about their quality of life, about what's important to them, we talk to their friends and relatives to try and build up a picture of the person.
On Wednesday, I was in the operating theatre for ear, nose and throat surgery on adults. You and the surgeon have a very close working partnership, because you want to be in the same space – the patient's head, where I am monitoring breathing, and the surgeon is operating. I like to meet the patient and explain what will happen, get them through surgery, wake them up, and make sure they are well enough to go home. For many, it's their first experience of surgery and they worry about the anaesthetic; there are minor operations, but there's no such thing as a minor anaesthetic.
