This year's FrightFest, the 15th, is the biggest ever, with a staggering 64 features scheduled over its four-and-a-half-day schedule. Most of those titles, however, will fall within certain predictable parameters that both define and delimit the genre. Before even looking at the programme, you can guess, accurately, that there will be plenty of murders by numbers, depravity on demand, rape-revenge routines and old tunes played by old devils. Near-interchangeable monsters will all be vying for the same terrain of the unconscious, the uncanny or the apocalyptic. This is, after all, the very flesh and blood of horror.
With these can be contrasted the occasional oddities, outliers and avant-garde offerings fostered in the side programmes or midnight slots. Existing on the festival margins, these are the films that either stand alone as sweet singularities, or else, precisely by being sui generis, redefine and expand the genre's boundaries. This year's FrightFest has its own small but significant set of category-defying curiosities, accessible – at least in one sense of the word – to anyone willing to hunt them down.
Jason Bognacki's Another, here in its European premiere, reimagines the witchy weirdness of Dario Argento's classic Suspiria (1977) while further fragmenting its own coming-of-age narrative into a fever dream of spatio-temporal ruptures and hyper-stylised imagery.
Matthew A Brown's Julia, also a European premiere, sends a horrifically abused woman's angry feminist(ish) awakening on a paranoid spin through cult conspiracy.
Alan Moore-scripted … Show Pieces
In the world premiere of Mitch Jenkins' tripartite Show Pieces, screenwriter Alan Moore rewrites the Egyptian Book of the Dead in the language of David Lynch, remapping nocturnal Northampton as the club scene for death in all its mystery – and localised banality.
There's a revival of The Visitor (1979), by Michael J Paradise (aka Guilio Paradisi), a lysergic blurring of science fiction and theology that sees an eternal conflict between good and evil manifesting itself in the contemporary cityscape of Atlanta, Georgia. Its stars include an unlikely trinity of directors: John Huston, Mel Ferrer and Sam Peckinpah!
The most mannered and strange – and therefore innovative and interesting – screening at this year's FrightFest is Hitoshi Matsumoto's R100. Like Takashi Miike's ground-breaking Audition (1999 – featured in the first FrightFest in 2000), R100 follows a middle-aged protagonist struggling to reconcile widowerhood and fatherhood with an abiding, guilt-tinged desire for sexual pleasure - except that Matsumoto impossibly reconfigures Takashi's transgressively shocking passions and self-torments into a bizarre sex comedy, full of postmodern self-awareness and surreal escapism.
